#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642503 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle -2\\cos \\theta\\sin \\theta\\left(\\frac{\\cos \\theta}{\\sin \\theta}+\\frac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta}\\right).\n$$ Assume $\\sin \\theta\\ne0$ and $\\cos \\theta\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& -2\\cos \\theta\\sin \\theta\\left(\\frac{\\cos \\theta}{\\sin \\theta}+\\frac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta}\\right) \\\\ &= -2\\cos \\theta\\sin \\theta\\cdot\\frac{\\cos \\theta}{\\sin \\theta} \\\\ &\\quad {}-2\\cos \\theta\\sin \\theta\\cdot\\frac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 \\theta$ and $\\cos^2 \\theta$ terms and simplify any matching factors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& -2\\cos \\theta\\sin \\theta\\cdot\\frac{\\cos \\theta}{\\sin \\theta} \\\\ &\\quad {}-2\\cos \\theta\\sin \\theta\\cdot\\frac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta} \\\\ &= -2(\\cos^{2} \\theta+\\sin^{2} \\theta)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2 \\theta+\\cos^2 \\theta=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& -2(\\cos^{2} \\theta+\\sin^{2} \\theta) \\\\ &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& -2\\cos \\theta\\sin \\theta\\left(\\frac{\\cos \\theta}{\\sin \\theta}+\\frac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta}\\right) \\\\ &= -2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642521 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin t(3\\sin t+3\\frac{1}{\\sin t})+3\\cos^{2} t.$ Assume $\\sin t\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\sin t(3\\sin t+3\\frac{1}{\\sin t}) \\\\ &\\quad {}+3\\cos^{2} t \\\\ &= 3\\sin^{2} t \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\sin t\\cdot\\frac{3}{\\sin t} \\\\ &\\quad {}+3\\cos^{2} t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 t$ and $\\cos^2 t$ terms and simplify any matching factors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& 3\\sin^{2} t \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\sin t\\cdot\\frac{3}{\\sin t} \\\\ &\\quad {}+3\\cos^{2} t \\\\ &= 3(\\sin^{2} t+\\cos^{2} t)+3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& 3(\\sin^{2} t+\\cos^{2} t)+3 \\\\ &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\sin t(3\\sin t+3\\frac{1}{\\sin t}) \\\\ &\\quad {}+3\\cos^{2} t \\\\ &= 6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642524 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\cos \\theta(2-4\\cos \\theta)-4\\sin^{2} \\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\cos \\theta(2-4\\cos \\theta)-4\\sin^{2} \\theta \\\\ &= - 4 \\sin^{2} \\theta- 4 \\cos^{2} \\theta \\\\ &\\quad {}+ 2 \\cos \\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 \\theta$ and $\\cos^2 \\theta$ terms and simplify any matching factors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& - 4 \\sin^{2} \\theta- 4 \\cos^{2} \\theta \\\\ &\\quad {}+ 2 \\cos \\theta \\\\ &= -4(\\cos^{2} \\theta+\\sin^{2} \\theta)+2\\cos \\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2 \\theta+\\cos^2 \\theta=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& -4(\\cos^{2} \\theta+\\sin^{2} \\theta)+2\\cos \\theta \\\\ &= 2 \\cos \\theta- 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\cos \\theta(2-4\\cos \\theta)-4\\sin^{2} \\theta \\\\ &= 2 \\cos \\theta- 4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642526 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $-4\\sin^{2} t\\left(1+\\frac{\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{2} t}\\right).$ Assume $\\sin t\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& -4\\sin^{2} t\\left(1+\\frac{\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{2} t}\\right) \\\\ &= -4\\sin^{2} t \\\\ &\\quad {}-4\\sin^{2} t\\cdot\\frac{\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{2} t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 t$ and $\\cos^2 t$ terms and simplify any matching factors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& -4\\sin^{2} t \\\\ &\\quad {}-4\\sin^{2} t\\cdot\\frac{\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{2} t} \\\\ &= -4(\\sin^{2} t+\\cos^{2} t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& -4(\\sin^{2} t+\\cos^{2} t) \\\\ &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& -4\\sin^{2} t\\left(1+\\frac{\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{2} t}\\right) \\\\ &= -4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642529 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\cos x(6\\cos x-10\\frac{1}{\\cos x})+6\\sin^{2} x.$ Assume $\\cos x\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\cos x(6\\cos x-10\\frac{1}{\\cos x}) \\\\ &\\quad {}+6\\sin^{2} x \\\\ &= 6\\cos^{2} x \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\cos x\\cdot\\frac{-10}{\\cos x} \\\\ &\\quad {}+6\\sin^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 x$ and $\\cos^2 x$ terms and simplify any matching factors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& 6\\cos^{2} x \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\cos x\\cdot\\frac{-10}{\\cos x} \\\\ &\\quad {}+6\\sin^{2} x \\\\ &= 6(\\cos^{2} x+\\sin^{2} x)-10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2 x+\\cos^2 x=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& 6(\\cos^{2} x+\\sin^{2} x)-10 \\\\ &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\cos x(6\\cos x-10\\frac{1}{\\cos x}) \\\\ &\\quad {}+6\\sin^{2} x \\\\ &= -4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642541 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $4\\cdot \\sin \\theta+\\frac{4\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\theta}{\\sin \\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "Put both terms over the common denominator $\\sin \\theta$, and combine the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{4\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\theta}{\\sin \\theta}+\\frac{4\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\theta}{\\sin \\theta} \\\\ & =\\frac{4\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\theta+4\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\theta}{\\sin \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGroup the squared terms and use $\\cos^{2} \\theta+\\sin^{2} \\theta=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{4\\cdot (\\cos^{2} \\theta+\\sin^{2} \\theta)}{\\sin \\theta} \\\\ & =\\frac{4}{\\sin \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe simplified expression is $\\frac{4}{\\sin \\theta}$, where $\\sin \\theta\\ne 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642546 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $4\\cdot \\cos \\theta+\\frac{2\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\theta}{\\cos \\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "Put both terms over the common denominator $\\cos \\theta$, and combine the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{4\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\theta}{\\cos \\theta}+\\frac{2\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\theta}{\\cos \\theta} \\\\ & =\\frac{4\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\theta+2\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\theta}{\\cos \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGroup the squared terms and use $\\sin^{2} \\theta+\\cos^{2} \\theta=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{2\\cdot (\\sin^{2} \\theta+\\cos^{2} \\theta)+2\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\theta}{\\cos \\theta} \\\\ & =\\frac{2+2\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\theta}{\\cos \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe simplified expression is $\\frac{2+2\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\theta}{\\cos \\theta}$, where $\\cos \\theta\\ne 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642554 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $3\\cdot \\sin t+\\frac{4\\cdot \\cos^{2} t}{\\sin t}.$" :question/worked-solution "Put both terms over the common denominator $\\sin t$, and combine the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{3\\cdot \\sin^{2} t}{\\sin t}+\\frac{4\\cdot \\cos^{2} t}{\\sin t} \\\\ & =\\frac{3\\cdot \\sin^{2} t+4\\cdot \\cos^{2} t}{\\sin t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGroup the squared terms and use $\\cos^{2} t+\\sin^{2} t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{4\\cdot (\\cos^{2} t+\\sin^{2} t)-\\sin^{2} t}{\\sin t} \\\\ & =\\frac{4-\\sin^{2} t}{\\sin t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe simplified expression is $\\frac{4-\\sin^{2} t}{\\sin t}$, where $\\sin t\\ne 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642560 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\cos x+\\frac{\\sin^{2} x}{\\cos x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Put both terms over the common denominator $\\cos x$, and combine the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{\\cos^{2} x}{\\cos x}+\\frac{\\sin^{2} x}{\\cos x} \\\\ & =\\frac{\\cos^{2} x+\\sin^{2} x}{\\cos x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGroup the squared terms and use $\\sin^{2} x+\\cos^{2} x=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{(\\sin^{2} x+\\cos^{2} x)}{\\cos x} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe simplified expression is $\\frac{1}{\\cos x}$, where $\\cos x\\ne 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642562 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\cos \\alpha+\\frac{2\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}.$" :question/worked-solution "Put both terms over the common denominator $\\cos \\alpha$, and combine the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{\\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}+\\frac{2\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha} \\\\ & =\\frac{\\cos^{2} \\alpha+2\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGroup the squared terms and use $\\sin^{2} \\alpha+\\cos^{2} \\alpha=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{2\\cdot (\\sin^{2} \\alpha+\\cos^{2} \\alpha)-\\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha} \\\\ & =\\frac{2-\\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe simplified expression is $\\frac{2-\\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}$, where $\\cos \\alpha\\ne 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642580 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{1+2\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}+4\\cdot \\tan \\alpha.$" :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the second term using $\\tan \\alpha=\\frac{\\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{1+2\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}+4\\cdot \\tan \\alpha \\\\ &= \\frac{1+2\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}+\\frac{4\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPut both terms over a common denominator and combine the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{(1+2\\cdot \\cos \\alpha)\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}+\\frac{4\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha} \\\\ &= \\frac{\\cos \\alpha+2\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\alpha+4\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGroup the squared terms and apply the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{\\cos \\alpha+4\\cdot (\\cos^{2} \\alpha+\\sin^{2} \\alpha)-2\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha} \\\\ &= \\frac{\\cos \\alpha+4-2\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere we used $\\sin^{2} \\alpha+\\cos^{2} \\alpha=1$. Therefore, the simplified expression is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos \\alpha+4-2\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha\\cdot \\cos \\alpha},\n$$ on the original domain $\\sin \\alpha\\ne0, \\cos \\alpha\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642585 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{2+3\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}+\\cot \\alpha.$" :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the second term using $\\cot \\alpha=\\frac{\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{2+3\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}+\\cot \\alpha \\\\ &= \\frac{2+3\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}+\\frac{\\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPut both terms over a common denominator and combine the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{(2+3\\cdot \\sin \\alpha)\\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}+\\frac{\\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot \\sin \\alpha+3\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha+\\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGroup the squared terms and apply the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{2\\cdot \\sin \\alpha+(\\sin^{2} \\alpha+\\cos^{2} \\alpha)+2\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot \\sin \\alpha+1+2\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere we used $\\cos^{2} \\alpha+\\sin^{2} \\alpha=1$. Therefore, the simplified expression is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\cdot \\sin \\alpha+1+2\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha},\n$$ on the original domain $\\cos \\alpha\\ne0, \\sin \\alpha\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642591 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{3\\cdot \\sin t}{1-\\cos t}-2\\cdot \\cot t.$" :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the second term using $\\cot t=\\frac{\\cos t}{\\sin t}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{3\\cdot \\sin t}{1-\\cos t}-2\\cdot \\cot t \\\\ &= \\frac{3\\cdot \\sin t}{1-\\cos t}+\\frac{-2\\cdot \\cos t}{\\sin t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPut both terms over a common denominator and combine the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{3\\cdot \\sin^{2} t}{(1-\\cos t)\\sin t}+\\frac{-2\\cdot (\\cos t(1-\\cos t))}{(1-\\cos t)\\sin t} \\\\ &= \\frac{3\\cdot \\sin^{2} t-2\\cdot \\cos t+2\\cdot \\cos^{2} t}{(1-\\cos t)\\sin t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGroup the squared terms and apply the Pythagorean identity. Then factor the numerator and cancel the common linear factor, retaining the original denominator exclusions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{3\\cdot (\\sin^{2} t+\\cos^{2} t)-2\\cdot \\cos t-\\cos^{2} t}{(1-\\cos t)\\sin t} \\\\ &= \\frac{3-2\\cdot \\cos t-\\cos^{2} t}{(1-\\cos t)\\sin t} \\\\ &= \\frac{3\\cdot (1-\\cos t)+(\\cos t(1-\\cos t))}{(1-\\cos t)\\sin t} \\\\ &= \\frac{(1-\\cos t)(3+\\cos t)}{(1-\\cos t)\\sin t} \\\\ &= \\frac{3+\\cos t}{\\sin t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere we used $\\cos^{2} t+\\sin^{2} t=1$. Therefore, the simplified expression is $\\frac{3+\\cos t}{\\sin t}$, on the original domain $\\sin t\\ne0, 1-\\cos t\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643298 :question/problem "![](images/bd/bddd8b3ba7352cd58667179d8b01c9c7f0ef523e4646e7c459ab577023356b64.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=6$ and the vertical radius has length $b=5.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643302 :question/problem "![](images/0b/0bbf58f80060415a5f773150d354f0548fbb37f9617929e4f86929e78ef1c572.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643312 :question/problem "![](images/4e/4eb7e5fed25a147865a356a491c551d745f58730ad9c3fe4c333c6f2e0d1cfca.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=9$ and the vertical radius has length $b=5.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{9}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643313 :question/problem "![](images/9c/9c992df95e92ddb3897feb6503bcde61cc7b38866584ca0153451928ec6a00a8.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=9$ and the vertical radius has length $b=7.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{9}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643325 :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd5fe8314e19850aaa2650cc0cc05b1ba959f20679b4406ce2e8d3fe4985af0f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=6$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643336 :question/problem "![](images/d9/d96b8c0c94500417798e1d80d51ae4a1dfddc6999c77f5ae693700e964c0d362.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643339 :question/problem "![](images/88/88a49f4f557fed52625353cacc258b6b7b4914b82d8f46867970ebf389afcb66.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $b=7.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643344 :question/problem "![](images/fc/fcf31a54300895f655eb1f46e732290416612e84786f40cdeeb7fa3080337c12.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=4$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643348 :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab6a640488e5b20fa205260aeb31fbcf6c49428087b2a62277a9e58118f3e850.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$ and the vertical radius has length $b=7.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643349 :question/problem "![](images/4f/4f2a5176bc5674bf5d755e0659aac28d7a2f9d2c7bf6af08c49a3f38658a6364.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=7.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643388 :question/problem "![](images/be/be3016908a3fba5debdfa3ac512e1a8871916fa68d2b9852d82b8fbfc7ea75f5.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(2,0)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643393 :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e347eee8f744be052197ac9461905e3d7eb89ea6fdaf61ce5a2299a0176c059.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(-4,7)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=5.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-4))^{2}+(y-7)^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y-7)^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643396 :question/problem "![](images/36/36b49f689c735249b87414fb3d994d31ec84479082bd8b1b4a1dce70d9050b74.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(-4,3)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-4))^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643404 :question/problem "![](images/7d/7dfe9e153e6db4864473b9522bcfe1ae511bce97154e26b4e08050c7125ddcf4.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(-5,0)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-5))^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643415 :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9ff685b5298b604bd7fd03012e98a2237346e4eade41c27d0561d9f2b540db3.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(0,-6)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+(y-(-6))^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y+6)^{2} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643421 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x+5)^{2}+(y-9)^{2}=2$?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+(y-9)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-(-5))^{2}+(y-9)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{2})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=-5,$ $b=9,$ and $r=\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-5,9)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643425 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=49$?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =49 \\\\ (x-(-3))^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & ={7}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=-3,$ $b=4,$ and $r=7.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-3,4)$ and its radius is $7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643429 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x-6)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=100$?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-6)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =100 \\\\ (x-6)^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2} & ={10}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=6,$ $b=-2,$ and $r=10.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(6,-2)$ and its radius is $10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643431 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x-1)^{2}+(y-10)^{2}=4$?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-10)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-10)^{2} & ={2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=1,$ $b=10,$ and $r=2.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(1,10)$ and its radius is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643447 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x+1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=2$?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{2})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=-1,$ $b=3,$ and $r=\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-1,3)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643454 :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{51}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{20}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=51$ and ${b}^{2}=20.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=51,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{51}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=20,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=\\sqrt{51},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{51}=2\\sqrt{51},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}=4\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643456 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{88}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{56}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=88$ and ${b}^{2}=56.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=88,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{88}=2\\sqrt{22}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=56,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{56}=2\\sqrt{14}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{22},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{14}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{22}=4\\sqrt{22},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{14}=4\\sqrt{14}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643462 :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{30}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=30$ and ${b}^{2}=2.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=30,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{30}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=2,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=\\sqrt{30},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{30}=2\\sqrt{30},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643478 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=100$ and ${b}^{2}=49.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=100,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{100}=10.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=49,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=10,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=7.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 10=20,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 7=14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643484 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{33}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{61}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=33$ and ${b}^{2}=61.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=33,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{33}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=61,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{61}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=\\sqrt{61},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{33}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{61}=2\\sqrt{61},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{33}=2\\sqrt{33}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186644239 :question/problem "![](images/da/dafe3c837094d2b683e6d67b7ea267b4105935b47ebcc5f9e5c05a6cb67bfa69.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-2,4)$ and $(-6,4)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-2,4)$ and $Y(-6,4),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{((-2)-(-6))^2+(4-4)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^2+(0)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{-2+(-6)}{2},\\frac{4+4}{2})=(-4,4)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=2$ and center $(a,b)=(-4,4),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-(-4))^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =(2)^2 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186644243 :question/problem "![](images/65/65981cc51d368435e242beb73049477b2ec4e1f66a6873391e2b80562f37afc9.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(3,6)$ and $(3,2)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(3,6)$ and $Y(3,2),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(3-3)^2+(6-2)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(4)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{3+3}{2},\\frac{6+2}{2})=(3,4)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=2$ and center $(a,b)=(3,4),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =(2)^2 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
